1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,360
As the story goes,

2
00:00:01,360 --> 00:00:04,381
when colonial officials in Delhi offered

3
00:00:04,381 --> 00:00:06,345
cash for every dead cobra,

4
00:00:06,345 --> 00:00:08,384
the city ended up with even

5
00:00:08,384 --> 00:00:09,970
more venomous snakes.

6
00:00:09,970 --> 00:00:12,634
Locals started breeding cobras in backyard

7
00:00:12,634 --> 00:00:14,727
sheds just to collect the payout.

8
00:00:14,727 --> 00:00:17,263
When officials discovered the scheme and

9
00:00:17,263 --> 00:00:18,532
canceled the bounty,

10
00:00:18,532 --> 00:00:21,259
breeders released the worthless snakes into

11
00:00:21,259 --> 00:00:22,020
the streets.

12
00:00:22,020 --> 00:00:22,596
In 1975,

13
00:00:22,596 --> 00:00:26,050
British economist Charles Goodhart observed that

14
00:00:26,050 --> 00:00:27,849
a number used as a target

15
00:00:27,849 --> 00:00:30,152
stops measuring what it used to.

16
00:00:30,152 --> 00:00:33,966
Anthropologist Marilyn Strathern later put it simply:

17
00:00:33,966 --> 00:00:36,269
when a measure becomes a target,

18
00:00:36,269 --> 00:00:38,500
it ceases to be a good measure.

19
00:00:38,500 --> 00:00:40,601
You fall into the same trap

20
00:00:40,601 --> 00:00:43,247
whenever you chase hours logged at

21
00:00:43,247 --> 00:00:43,792
a desk,

22
00:00:43,792 --> 00:00:44,726
app streaks,

23
00:00:44,726 --> 00:00:47,139
or vanity views instead of real

24
00:00:47,139 --> 00:00:49,240
skills and cashflow margin.

25
00:00:49,240 --> 00:00:51,425
Use numbers as a quiet compass,

26
00:00:51,425 --> 00:00:53,046
never as your identity,

27
00:00:53,046 --> 00:00:55,442
because the second you worship the

28
00:00:55,442 --> 00:00:58,050
scoreboard you start gaming yourself.
